arithmetic packets, Galois-to-Weil--Deligne attachment, coefficient and
Hodge--Tate/period comparisons, and arithmetic Frobenius coverage remain
outside this result. At the Block 19 checkpoint, the ledger recorded fifteen
checked claims and five partial claims; see the Block 23 conditional-scope
correction above. Universal occupancy remains conjectural.


\paragraph{General finite logarithmic recovery (Block 20).}
Let $A$ be a rational algebra, without any commutativity assumption on $A$.
For a unipotent element $U$ and a positive integer $d$ satisfying
$(U-1)^d=0$, put
\[
L_d(U)=\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}{j}(U-1)^j.
\]
The value is independent of the chosen annihilating exponent. The finite
logarithm and the nilpotent exponential satisfy
\[
L(\exp N)=N,\qquad \exp(L(U))=U.
\]
Both maps preserve every annihilating exponent, and hence preserve the
exact nilpotency class of $N$ and $\exp(N)-1$, or of $U-1$ and $L(U)$.
The Lean finite sum includes its zero-index term with coefficient zero;
the displayed mathematical sum starts at one. The formal statements also
handle the zero algebra and the exponent-zero case.

The proof evaluates Mathlib's formal power-series inverse identities at
constant nilpotent series. Such evaluation is exactly a finite polynomial.
For an arbitrary noncommutative ambient algebra, perform the calculation
inside the commutative subalgebra generated by the element and then apply
its inclusion. No analytic convergence or topology is assumed. The
finite logarithm commutes with ring homomorphisms between rational algebras.

Let $K$ be a characteristic-zero field and $N$ an endomorphism with
$N^d=0$. For $t\ne0$ the actual recovery formula is
\[
N=\frac1t\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}{j}
       \bigl(\exp(tN)-1\bigr)^j.
\]
All coefficients lie in $K$. The vector spaces need not be finite
dimensional, but nilpotence still means that some finite power of the
whole operator is zero. A linear map intertwines two nilpotent operators exactly
when it intertwines their exponential samples at a common nonzero
parameter. Also
\[
\ker\bigl(\exp(tN)-1\bigr)=\ker N.
\]

For supplied covariance data and the twisted cocycle of Block 13, let
$\rho_D(g)=\exp(t(g)N_D)r_D(g)$. If a common element $u$ satisfies
$r_D(u)=r_E(u)=1$ and $t(u)\ne0$, every intertwiner of $\rho_D$ and
$\rho_E$ recovers both the original $r$-intertwiner and the $N$-intertwiner.
There is no cubic nilpotence restriction. This recovers equivariant
projectors for both original components. Reconstructed-action isomorphisms
induce actual isomorphisms of the inertia-fixed monodromy kernels. Their local characteristic polynomials
agree under the existing finite-dimensional and normal-subgroup hypotheses.

For actual finite original inertia images the common kernel sample is
derived. Suppose $u\in H$ satisfies $\nu(u)=1$ and $t(u)\ne0$ and both
$r_D(H)$ and $r_E(H)$ are finite. Their sampled actions have positive
finite orders $a,b$. At $u^{ab}$ both original actions are trivial, and
\[
t(u^{ab})=(ab)t(u)\ne0
\]
by the cocycle identity and characteristic zero. Thus the same full
intertwiner equivalence holds using the actual finite-image hypotheses,
without a separately supplied common trivial-action sample. The initial
nonzero cocycle sample and the arithmetic interpretation of the data
remain requirements.

Actual affine representations with monodromy chains of every finite
length invoke these theorems. In particular a length-six example has
$N^5\ne0$ and $N^6=0$ and is recovered from its actual reconstructed action.
The length-four example still defeats the old quadratic logarithm, while
the full finite logarithm recovers it.

The sample hypotheses retain substantive content. A zero parameter loses
$N$. For unrestricted supplied algebraic data, one can also absorb
$\exp(tN)$ into $r$ and set the new monodromy to zero; the reconstructed
representation is unchanged. The absorbed original action fails the
required trivial-action sample condition. This is a counterexample in the
algebraic datum class, not a failure of uniqueness of arithmetic
Weil--Deligne attachment with finite original inertia action.

Block 20 removes the general logarithmic-extraction gap from the local
algebraic comparison. It does not construct the actual arithmetic
attachment, identify every intended packet, prove the coefficient and
Hodge--Tate/period comparisons, or prove arithmetic Frobenius coverage.
At the Block 20 checkpoint, the ledger recorded fifteen checked claims
and five partial claims; see the Block 23 scope correction above.



\paragraph{Interpreted quadratic arithmetic completion (Block 21).}
Fix the continuous quotient
\[
q:G_{\Q}\longrightarrow C_2
\]
obtained by the action on a chosen square root $i$ of $-1$ in the algebraic
closure. Its surjectivity and the equivalence
\[
q(\sigma)=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\sigma(i)=i
\]
are proved using the existing actual quadratic Galois construction.
For a commutative ring $B$, define the arithmetic packet
\[
\mathcal P_q(B)=
\{\chi:G_{\Q}\longrightarrow B^\times\mid\ker q\subseteq\ker\chi\},
\]
where the maps are group homomorphisms. Unique descent through the actual
surjective map $q$ is derived, rather than supplied. Every member has finite
image and is continuous for the coefficient topology, since its descent
has the finite discrete domain $C_2$.

Let $R$ be any commutative ring and $B$ any commutative $R$-algebra. The
coordinate ring $A_R=R[X]/(X^2-1)$ represents precisely this specified
packet:
\[
\Hom_{R\text{-alg}}(A_R,B)
\simeq\{x\in B:x^2=1\}
\simeq\Hom(C_2,B^\times)
\simeq\mathcal P_q(B).
\]
The first map evaluates the coordinate. Its inverse is the quotient
universal property. A root is a unit with itself as inverse and determines
a character by its value on the generator of $C_2$. Finally pull back
along $q$. All inverse identities and naturality under coefficient
homomorphisms are proved. In particular, the point reductions and actual
arithmetic character reductions coincide. There is no invertibility-of-two,
reducedness or nontriviality hypothesis on $B$. No smoothness or
\'{e}taleness statement at two is used or asserted.

Let $I\subseteq R$ and suppose that $R$ is $I$-adically complete. Using
the checked ring-map reconstruction on the quadratic coordinate ring,
Lean proves
\[
\mathcal P_q(R)\simeq\varprojlim_{m\ge0}\mathcal P_q(R/I^m).
\]
The zero-precision quotient is included only for the formal indexing.
The inverse constructs an actual arithmetic character, with exactly the
prescribed reductions. It is unique, and its continuity is derived from
its factorization through the same finite quotient.

There is also a completion theorem without chosen compatible characters.
Suppose that $R/I^m$ is finite for every $m\ge1$, and that
$C_m\subseteq\mathcal P_q(R/I^m)$ are allowed subsets such that every
reduction of an allowed member is allowed. Then
\[
\begin{split}
&\exists\chi\in\mathcal P_q(R),\quad
  \chi\text{ is continuous and }\chi\bmod I^m\in C_m
  \text{ for all }m\ge1\\
&\hspace{3em}\Longleftrightarrow\quad
  C_m\ne\varnothing\text{ for all }m\ge1.
\end{split}
\]
The root interpretation makes the allowed finite levels genuinely finite.
Finite-branch compactness chooses the compatible roots, and the ring
reconstruction gives their integral root. Naturality turns this into the
required arithmetic character. Surjectivity of transitions and individual
liftability of every finite node are not assumed. The root-level theorem
needs only the allowed root subsets to be finite.

For $R=\Z_2$, Lean derives finiteness of all positive maximal-ideal
quotients and applies this completion theorem. Since $\Z_2$ is a domain,
its only roots of $X^2-1$ are $1$ and $-1$. Thus the integral packet has
exactly two distinct characters: the trivial character and the sign
character describing the action on $i$. They have the same reduction
modulo two and distinct reductions modulo four.

The distinction between finite occupancy and continuation is witnessed
by an actual finite arithmetic character. The roots modulo eight are
$1,3,5,7$. The root $3$ defines a member of
$\mathcal P_q(\Z/8\Z)$, but no member of
$\mathcal P_q(\Z/16\Z)$ reduces to it. Indeed the only scalar lifts of
$3$ are $3$ and $11$, and both square to $9$ modulo sixteen. The formal
proof also classifies all roots modulo sixteen and the reductions with
children. Consequently a nonempty finite node need not lie on an
infinite branch, even in this explicitly interpreted arithmetic packet.

Block 21 completes this restricted arithmetic interpretation and its
conditional completion. The packet is defined by one fixed quotient; it
is not the collection of all quadratic characters of $G_{\Q}$. No local
ramification criterion, Hodge--Tate weight, Weil--Deligne attachment, or
cross-coefficient local invariant was established by Block 21. The full
manuscript still requires its remaining packet identifications and
arithmetic comparisons. At the Block 21 checkpoint, the ledger recorded
fifteen checked claims and five partial claims; see the Block 23 conditional-scope
correction above. Universal positive occupancy remains conjectural.

\paragraph{Actual quadratic local conditions and completion (Block 22).}
Continue with the fixed packet $\mathcal P_q$ above. For a
characteristic-zero field $K$, let
\[
j_K:G_K\longrightarrow G_{\Q}
\]
be the actual field-induced homomorphism between absolute Galois groups.
It includes a chosen embedding of algebraic closures. Let $i_K$ be the
image of the chosen square root $i$ under that embedding. Lean proves
compatibility with the Galois actions and hence
\[
(q\circ j_K)(\sigma)=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \sigma(i_K)=i_K.
\]
The fixed-field theorem for the algebraic closure then gives
\[
q\circ j_K=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists x\in K,\ x^2=-1.
\]
If no such root exists, the local quotient is surjective onto $C_2$.
These are statements about the actual local Galois map, not a supplied
arithmetic interpretation of a scalar label.

For every commutative coefficient ring $B$ and $\chi\in\mathcal P_q(B)$,
define $\chi_K=\chi\circ j_K$. The local character is continuous and
commutes with coefficient change. The precise local admissibility
condition is
\[
\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \chi_K=1.
\]
Descent through the fixed quadratic quotient proves
\[
\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\bigl(\exists x\in K,\ x^2=-1\bigr)\ \lor\ \chi=1.
\]
No reducedness, characteristic-zero or domain hypothesis is imposed on
$B$. In particular, the formula applies to the finite coefficient
quotients used in completion.

The p-adic square-root criterion is proved for every prime:
\[
\bigl(\exists x\in\Q_p,\ x^2=-1\bigr)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p\equiv1\pmod4.
\]
A root satisfies $\lvert x\rvert_p^2=1$ and is therefore integral.
Reduction modulo three rules out a root in $\Q_3$, and reduction modulo
four rules out a root in $\Q_2$. At an odd prime, any residue root of
$X^2+1$ has nonzero derivative and lifts by the pinned Mathlib Hensel
theorem. The finite-field square criterion then gives the stated
congruence. An explicit Hensel application at the residue root $2$ modulo $5$ supplies
a root in $\Z_5$. Consequently
\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_{\Q_p}(\chi)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p\equiv1\pmod4\ \lor\ \chi=1.
}
\]
The arithmetic prime $p$ is independent of the coefficient prime. For
the two integral $\Z_2$-valued characters, the sign character is locally
trivial exactly at primes congruent to one modulo four. Both distinct
global characters are locally trivial at five, while the sign character
is nontrivial on the full local Galois groups at three and two.

The inertia theorem uses actual residue actions. For a valuation subring
$A$ of an extension field $L/K$, let $D_A$ be its decomposition subgroup
in $\operatorname{Aut}_K(L)$ and put
\[
I_A=\ker\bigl(D_A\longrightarrow\operatorname{Aut}(k_A)\bigr),
\]
where $k_A$ is the residue field. If $2$ is a unit in $A$, every member
of $I_A$ fixes every $y\in L$ with $y^2=-1$. Indeed $y^{-1}=-y$, so
$y$ belongs to $A$ and is a unit there. An automorphism sends $y$ to
$y$ or $-y$. Their residues are distinct because $2$ and $y$ are units,
whereas inertia fixes residues. The second possibility is excluded.

For every prime $p$, Lean constructs a valuation subring
$A\subseteq\overline{\Q_p}$ containing the integral image of $\Z_p$
such that the induced map $\Z_p\to A$ is local. For odd $p$, two is
a unit. The preceding argument applies to every valuation subring
containing that integral image, and hence every member of
$\mathcal P_q(B)$ is trivial on its inertia subgroup after actual local
restriction. The existence theorem supplies a locally dominating choice,
so this assertion is not vacuous.

This inertia is the actual residue-action kernel inside $D_A$. No equality
$D_A=G_{\Q_p}$ is established in this block. Nor is ramification at two
proved: the nontrivial full local character there is a separate result.
At three the $\Z_2$-valued sign character is nontrivial on the full local
Galois group but trivial on every inertia subgroup covered by the theorem.
Thus local triviality and the stated inertia triviality are distinguished
by checked arithmetic examples.

The local conditions survive coefficient completion by a proved theorem.
For an $I$-adically separated commutative ring $R$,
\[
\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\operatorname{LocallyTrivial}_K(\chi\bmod I^m)
\text{ for every }m\ge1.
\]
If $-1$ has a square root in $K$, every packet member satisfies the
condition. Otherwise the condition says that the character is globally
trivial, and separatedness detects that equality through all reductions.
This proves the closure statement for the independently defined arithmetic
property.

Suppose now that $R$ is $I$-adically complete and that every positive
quotient $R/I^m$ is finite. Let $C_m\subseteq\mathcal P_q(R/I^m)$ be
allowed subsets preserved by reduction, and let $(p_s)_{s\in S}$ be any
indexed family of arithmetic primes. The index set $S$ need not be finite.
Lean proves the simultaneous arithmetic completion criterion
\[
\begin{split}
&\exists\chi\in\mathcal P_q(R),\quad
 \chi\text{ continuous},\quad
 \chi\bmod I^m\in C_m\ (m\ge1),\quad
 \chi_{\Q_{p_s}}=1\ (s\in S)\\
&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \forall m\ge1,\ \exists\psi\in C_m,\quad
 \forall s\in S,\ p_s\equiv1\pmod4\ \lor\ \psi=1.
\end{split}
\]
The constructed character also satisfies every odd-prime valuation-inertia
condition stated above. Finite-branch compactness selects all finite
characters together; no coherent branch or surjective transition maps
are assumed. The new local-closure implication is proved, not supplied.
Every-level occupancy remains a substantive hypothesis. One nonsplit
selected prime forces the completed global character to be trivial.

For the integral dyadic packet, a single detecting precision is also
proved: reduction modulo four detects local triviality uniformly in the
arithmetic prime. Modulo two does not suffice. The sign character is
nontrivial locally at three, while its reduction modulo two is trivial.
This is a coefficient-depth failure, distinct from splitting of the
arithmetic field.

Block 22 finishes this actual local admissibility and completion step for
the fixed quadratic packet. It does not identify all packets in the
manuscript's common arithmetic spine, attach Weil--Deligne objects, or
prove coefficient and Hodge--Tate/period comparisons. The decomposition
and full local Galois group identification remains a separate inertia
obligation, and ramification at two is not claimed. The full arithmetic
ledger at the Block 22 checkpoint recorded fifteen checked source claims
and five partial claims; the Block 23 conditional-scope correction gives
sixteen checked and four partial.
Universal occupancy and natural finite seed rank remain conjectures.

