\begin{abstract}
Let \(\bar\rho\) be a residual Galois representation and let \(R\) be a global deformation ring with declared local conditions. We separate the global automorphy problem into residual occupancy, deformation support, Hecke occupancy, and arithmetic realization. Local conditions are encoded by quotient rings rather than by idempotents, since irreducible components of a local deformation ring need not be clopen. For an ordinary or derived patching datum, full support descends through the augmentation quotient. Near faithfulness is shown to be equivalent to full support for finite modules over Noetherian rings. An isomorphism \(R\cong\TT\) transports support, Fitting ideals, and point fibers, but a quotient map can erase an entire component. Finite Hecke algebras are resolved into canonical Artin packets, so simultaneous generalized eigenspaces are available without assuming semisimplicity. Nested packets in one finite carrier stabilize; for changing finite carriers, nonzero stable cores give compatible inverse limit vectors. These results give a pointwise criterion: a characteristic zero deformation point in automorphic support produces a Hecke eigensystem. With attached Galois representations, local global compatibility, and classicality, the point is automorphic. A fixed compatible system then propagates the conclusion to every coefficient place. For finite coefficient quotients, compatible conjugacy classes admit compatible continuous representatives. Declared polynomial quotient conditions survive their adic reconstruction. These completion statements require explicit finite occupancy, continuity, and integral Frobenius data. Universal companion existence and unrestricted reciprocity are not proved.
\end{abstract}
\makeatletter\@setabstract\makeatother
\thispagestyle{plain}

\section*{Publication and supporting files}
This reading edition, dated 10 October 2026, retains the complete revised mathematical proofs. Fourteen inherited vector figures are unchanged. Four diagrams have been redrawn to separate their arrows, expose the supplied inputs, and synchronize the final architecture with the five gates of Theorem~\ref{thm:five_gate_completion}. Supporting Markdown (\texttt{.md}) files are included with the source: the publication note, formal-evidence boundary, original-to-revised claim crosswalk, current status, source-preservation ledger, and build instructions. They record the statement hypotheses, evidence scope, and editorial changes alongside the preserved original and revised source snapshots.

The saved release reports complete mapped formal evidence for all forty-nine revised internal claims, while the original-wording census is forty-one complete, seven partial, and one unchecked. These are different contracts. The completed tensor and point-factorization results are algebraic deductions for supplied deformation data; representability, residual occupancy, applicable patching and classicality theorems, attached Galois representations, Frobenius density, semisimplification, and the general Brauer--Nesbitt theorem remain external arithmetic or algebraic inputs. Neither Paper XI's general Conjecture~66 nor universal companion existence is assumed.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Automorphy lifting joins several kinds of mathematics that are easy to conflate. A residual representation must first occur in an automorphic carrier. The chosen characteristic zero representation must lie on a deformation problem controlled by local conditions. A patching argument must show that the automorphic module reaches the relevant part of the deformation space. A comparison such as \(R\cong\TT\) must identify the arithmetic and Hecke coordinates. Finally, the resulting Hecke point must be classical and must carry the expected Galois representation with the required local compatibility.

None of these steps is merely a change of notation. A deeper lift above one residual representation cannot create a missing residual companion. Freeness over an auxiliary group algebra does not imply that every component of a deformation ring is occupied. An isomorphism of rings transports support that already exists, but it does not create a nonzero automorphic vector. A nonzero class in completed or torsion cohomology need not be classical without a theorem that says so.

The Taylor Wiles method and its later forms solve these problems in many important settings \cite{Wiles1995,TaylorWiles1995,Diamond1997,Kisin2009,Gee2022}. Higher dimensional and derived versions use a broader range of local deformation rings, residual image conditions, torsion classes, and cohomological carriers \cite{ClozelHarrisTaylor2008,BarnetLambGeeGeraghtyTaylor2014,CalegariGeraghty2018,Thorne2012,MiagkovThorne2023,Scholze2015,CaraianiScholze2017,NewtonThorne2016}. The present article does not replace those theorems. It isolates the algebraic support statements that explain exactly what a patching package supplies once its arithmetic hypotheses have been verified.

The first correction concerns local packets. A local deformation ring is usually complete and local. Its irreducible components are rarely open and closed. They cannot generally be represented by central idempotents. The correct object is a quotient ring defining a local condition, together with the support of a module or complex on the resulting spectrum. Central idempotents reappear later, where they belong, inside finite Artin Hecke algebras and finite dimensional characteristic zero carriers.

The second correction concerns patching. The useful global conclusion is full support, often expressed as near faithfulness. For a finite module over a Noetherian ring, near faithfulness is equivalent to full support. Full support descends through the quotient that removes the auxiliary variables. This statement is elementary, but it is the exact bridge from a patched module to componentwise occupancy at finite level. The same conclusion holds for perfect complexes through derived support.

The third correction concerns Hecke packets. Semisimplicity is not required to isolate a maximal Hecke packet. Every finite commutative algebra over a field is a product of Artin local algebras. Its canonical central idempotents project onto simultaneous generalized eigenspaces. Trace is only a secondary criterion in characteristic zero. Support and localization are the primary notions.

The last part assembles these statements into a conditional theorem. A characteristic zero point of \(R\) is automorphic when it lies in the support of the automorphic carrier, the comparison with the Hecke algebra is available, and the corresponding Hecke fiber satisfies the declared realization theorem. If the point belongs to a fixed strictly compatible system, equality of Frobenius polynomials propagates the automorphic identification to every coefficient place by Chebotarev density and Brauer Nesbitt \cite{BrauerNesbitt1941,Neukirch1999,Gee2022}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
